    "Reflected light: geometric albedo p is the same at every SPHEREx wavelength (grey), phase function 1 (no darkening), Sun a 5772 K blackbody anchored at J. Methane absorption at 1.7, 2.3 and 3.3 um in a real giant planet would make it fainter there.",
    "Thermal light: a blackbody at T = (T_eq^4 + T_int^4)^(1/4), emissivity 1, Bond albedo taken equal to p. Real cold giant atmospheres emit far more than a blackbody at 4-5 um (Fortney et al. 2016), so the thermal term is a lower bound.",
    "Viewing geometry: SPHEREx looks about 90 degrees from the Sun, so the planet is sqrt(r^2 - 1) AU from SPHEREx.",
    "A planet's spectrum is not flat. 'Conservative' completeness uses its faintest SPHEREx detector band (a lower bound), 'optimistic' its brightest (an upper bound).",
    "Brighter than the brightest SIMULATED magnitude (AB 15) we use the brightest bin's completeness only if it is already at least 50%; saturation of very bright objects was not studied.",
    "Planet Nine positions are for 2018-06-01 (the population file's epoch); the overlap with searched patches is a statistical estimate."
